FieldFiller — third-party notices and attribution
=================================================

This file is the attribution surface required by C-009. It is separate from
LICENSE, which is FieldFiller's own licence and covers FieldFiller's own code.


1. Bundled third-party code
---------------------------

None.

The published extension has no runtime dependencies. `package.json` declares no
`dependencies` field at all; everything under `devDependencies` is build and test
tooling that does not enter the shipped bundle. A CI gate walks the page agent's
import graph on every change and fails the build on any package that is not on an
explicit allowlist, so this section staying empty is enforced rather than
remembered.

No fonts, icons, images, corpora, or data files in this repository are taken from
a third-party source. The name and address corpus is original; the icons are
original artwork whose full provenance is the committed generator that draws
them.


2. Prior work: Fake Filler
--------------------------

FieldFiller is an independent reimplementation. It was designed after a detailed
study of Fake Filler, an MIT-licensed form-filling extension by Hussein Shabbir,
which established the product category and whose behaviour informed the
requirements this project was built against.

No Fake Filler source code has been copied into this repository. Every line here
was written for this project, and the design record (`docs/vision.md` §7.4)
documents seventeen decisions taken deliberately *against* the reference's
implementation. What was carried across is understanding of the problem — which
field types matter, why first-match-wins ordering beats specificity scoring, why
profiles should augment a global rule list rather than replace it — not
expression.

The notice below is reproduced in full nonetheless. MIT's condition attaches to
copies and substantial portions of the software, and reasonable people could
disagree about where study ends and derivation begins in a reimplementation this
closely informed by its predecessor. Reproducing the notice costs nothing and
settles the question in the direction of giving credit.

